#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook for mad-poco-powers: routes superpowers' brainstorming
# step to brainstorming-with-docs, routes shipping to ship-to-pr, and tells
# superpowers' workflow when to check its work against a PRD.
set -euo pipefail

read -r -d '' context <<'TEXT' || true
<IMPORTANT>
You also have mad-poco-powers, which complements superpowers.

- Wherever superpowers (including using-superpowers) tells you to use `superpowers:brainstorming`, invoke `mad-poco-powers:brainstorming-with-docs` instead. It is the same process (paths, hard gate, spec), driven by grilling rounds and keeping GLOSSARY.md and ADRs current. Never run both.
- When the user says to write the plan and implement it, to ship an approved design, or to open a PR for an approved design or for finished work, use `mad-poco-powers:ship-to-pr`: plan, subagent-driven implementation, PRD check, then a PR with a concise, complete description.
- When the user wants product requirements written down (a PRD, a product spec, what to build and why), use `mad-poco-powers:prd`. Its output feeds brainstorming-with-docs.
- When the user wants to be grilled or to stress-test a plan, use `mad-poco-powers:grilling`. It asks closed questions through AskUserQuestion and open ones in chat.
- `mad-poco-powers:domain-modeling` (used by brainstorming-with-docs and prd) and `mad-poco-powers:codebase-design` (used by brainstorming-with-docs) also apply directly when the user works on domain terms, ADRs, module interfaces or seams.

PRD checks: if the repo has a PRD in docs/prd/*.md (check at the moment it matters, since one can be written mid-session), it is the agreed product intent. Run `mad-poco-powers:prd-check`:
- after `superpowers:writing-plans` writes a plan, before execution starts;
- before `superpowers:finishing-a-development-branch` integrates a branch;
- whenever a change you make (a debugging fix, a review fix, a quick change) alters user-visible behavior the PRD covers.
It points out contradictions and extensions and asks the user whether to amend the PRD or change the work. While a plan is executing, do not stop to ask: follow the spec, which was already checked against the PRD, record any difference as a ruling, and ask at the check before integration.
</IMPORTANT>
TEXT

# Escape for JSON embedding (adapted from obra/superpowers' session-start hook, MIT).
escape_for_json() {
    local s="$1"
    s="${s//\\/\\\\}"
    s="${s//\"/\\\"}"
    s="${s//$'\n'/\\n}"
    s="${s//$'\r'/\\r}"
    s="${s//$'\t'/\\t}"
    printf '%s' "$s"
}

printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",\n    "additionalContext": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' "$(escape_for_json "$context")"
